36] [17592186049380 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428081 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049380 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428082 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049380 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428083 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049380 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428084 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049380 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428085 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049380 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428086 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049380 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428087 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049380 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving First-Order ODEs Using Direct Integration" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049380 #edb/ref 1033 1061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049380 #edb/ref 1034 1.0647300170873886 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049380 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "f00b1f48-2a5d-5edc-ba37-2d8457af2735" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050150 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050150 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050150 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050150 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050150 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050150 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050150 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050150 #edb/ref 1031 "Linear Independence of Solutions to Homogeneous ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050150 #edb/ref 1033 2547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050150 #edb/ref 1034 0.5791816603989243 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050150 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "3185fbd2-cc78-5eb1-ba66-6a65b74ad8a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050522 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050522 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050522 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050522 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050522 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050522 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050522 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428754 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050522 #edb/ref 1031 "General Solutions of Linear ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050522 #edb/ref 1033 2740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050522 #edb/ref 1034 0.722760783022951 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050522 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "503f6e39-70fd-54b8-a04b-77e9fcd7b979" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147227 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21778" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147227 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = x\\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\sin(4x)$ and $y_2(x) = \\cos(4x).$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n![](images/c5/c5c61b7abb13d72afcf6227c4e6223c3fcb2d3ba4603887e019c707a58dc3e78.svg)\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n![](images/a4/a4767e062ae48669a097695597586b93fd3c8d40ae4b829295f161986c2622b1.svg)\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n![](images/71/71d5f83043daf3c2b8012b57f4519873361caa6a87414fcdd6a95bb477b4157c.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating $u_1'$ and $u_2'{:}$\n\n![](images/98/98cd4d9d5d48d39b68ad528437417f4eb200ca988bec1199f6e4c473c0d76338.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147227 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "76b310bf-b592-541c-82e8-93d72bc688f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147227 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'.\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = \\sin(4x), \\qquad y_2 = \\cos(4x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p =u_1\\sin(4x)+u_2\\cos(4x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p',$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1(\\sin(4x))'+u_2 (\\cos(4x))'.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates $u_1'$ and $u_2'$ by differentiating $y_p$ and equating this expression to our constraint on $y_p'.$\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))}^{′}+{({u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+{u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for $y_p',$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & ={u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & =4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\cancel{4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-\\cancel{4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)} & =\\cancel{4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}-\\cancel{4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\nu_1' \\sin(4x) + u_2' \\cos(4x) = 0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147228 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21779" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147228 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 5y' - 6y = p(x),\n$$\n\nwhose corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x)=e^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n![](images/58/58c8777459adc6e01e911d6b195e594bf2f6eca10d391c92245fea108452f911.svg)\n\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n![](images/69/695f7685566bb01af237cd41a612210a8b35605cfb8197838c3b0d5d851299ae.svg)\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between $u_1', u_2',$ and $p(x){:}$\n\n![](images/16/16d0290d86015f14f76025dce0b24ce19b8de65238de2e34a614cd4d2e85406e.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147228 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8587baf6-8f11-56eb-8afa-dd385c0aeaeb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147228 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'.\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2 = e^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p =u_1e^{x}+u_2e^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p',$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' =u_1(e^{x})'+u_2 (e^{-6x})'.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-6y & =p(x) \\\\ ({y}_{p}^{′})^{′}+5{y}_{p}^{′}-6{y}_{p} & =p(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our expressions for $y_p$ and $y_p',$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-6x})^{′})}^{′}+5({u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-6x})^{′})-6({u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x}) & =p(x) \\\\ {({u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x})}^{′}+5{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-30{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x}-6{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x} & =p(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-6x}+36{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\,+5{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-30{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x}-6{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x} & =p(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group all terms proportional to $u_1$ and $u_2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}{{({e}^{x}+5{e}^{x}-6{e}^{x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{2}{{(36{e}^{-6x}-30{e}^{-6x}-6{e}^{-6x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-6x}=p(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the expressions in parentheses are both zero. Therefore, the equation reduces to\n\n$$\nu_1'e^{x} - 6u_2'e^{-6x} = p(x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147344 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6615" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147344 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' + 9y = 6\\cos x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\cos(3x)$ and $y_2 = \\sin(3x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x) y_1 + u_2(x) y_2\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle u_2(x) = \\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x,$ find the function $g.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147344 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb472181-da8e-5252-9c80-4d336e2ccf9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147344 #edb/ref 1074 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x) = 6\\cos x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos(3x), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)+3{sin}^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))}^{′}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for $u_2.$ We start by eliminating $u_1'$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\nu_1' = -\\dfrac{u_2'\\sin(3x)}{\\cos(3x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -3(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x) = 2\\cos x \\cos(3x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147487 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147487 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147487 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147487 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147487 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147487 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147487 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147487 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147487 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a VOP Equation by Substituting the Ansatz into the Original ODE" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147487 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186147228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147487 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5e92641d-b0bf-579b-960b-d6024d4b99b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147488 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147488 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147488 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186147777 #edb/ref 17592186045417