ref 17592186045417] [17592186147706 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147706 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147706 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147706 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147706 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147706 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147706 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186148385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147706 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147706 #edb/ref 1045 "Variation of Parameters for Second-Order ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147706 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186148377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147706 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147706 #edb/ref 1050 480.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186147706 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "2d0a4fd3-0b40-55d9-a65f-a3e25a834d56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148377 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148377 #edb/ref 1061 592933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148377 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "516c6bdd-8804-53ca-9908-eebaccdfcf76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148377 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148378 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148378 #edb/ref 1061 592934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148378 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "20c55f04-4f61-5ee4-89ed-3c45445b4316" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148378 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148379 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148379 #edb/ref 1061 592935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148379 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a5d506d3-aab1-5c01-a208-3aff0e9a1ae4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148379 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148380 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148380 #edb/ref 1061 592936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148380 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1cb0eaae-28c1-5cb0-b103-1a661d0a791b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148380 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148381 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148381 #edb/ref 1061 592939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148381 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "20bbfcce-b50e-5292-b54a-6d5b23b90624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148381 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148382 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148382 #edb/ref 1061 592940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148382 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d96ab13b-ff0b-5dba-9971-fbe7863a7466" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148382 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148383 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148383 #edb/ref 1061 592943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148383 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fc69b18f-972f-5cff-93c5-589c84582cff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148383 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148384 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186148385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148384 #edb/ref 1061 592944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148384 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "18c275f3-618a-5f6e-b41d-c032d969f4d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148384 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186147489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148385 #edb/ref 1061 592947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148385 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f378e6ad-cfc8-5fc2-9405-a55f070524dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148385 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186148655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148651 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's remind ourselves of our strategy for calculating $u_1$ and $u_2$.\n\n- **Step 1**: First, we substitute the above expressions for $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation. This will give one relationship between $u_1$ and $u_2.\\:{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we differentiate $y_p$ and equate the resulting expression to our constraint for $y_p'.$ This will give a second relationship between $u_1$ and $u_2.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Now that we have two equations for the two unknown functions, we solve the system to yield expressions for $u_1$ and $u_2.$\n\nNow that we've completed step 1 in some cases, let's go ahead and get some practice with step 2.\n\nFor example, consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' + y' - 6y = xe^{5x}.\n$$\n\nfor which the corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{-3x}.$ \n\nGiven a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'.\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = e^{2x}, \\qquad y_2 = e^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p = u_1e^{2x}+u_2e^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p',$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1(e^{2x})'+u_2 (e^{-3x})'.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates $u_1'$ and $u_2'$ by differentiating $y_p$ and equating this expression to our constraint on $y_p'.$\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{2x})}^{′}+{({u}_{2}{e}^{-3x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{1}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for $y_p',$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x} & ={u}_{1}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x} & =2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}-3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-\\cancel{3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}} & =\\cancel{2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{3{u}_{2}{e}^{-3x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\nu_1' e^{2x} + u_2' e^{-3x} = 0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148651 #edb/ref 1065 14603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148651 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c984d872-e2d6-5bc0-bd39-79c4e6c02172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148651 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding a Second Condition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148652 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, we’ll apply the method of variation of parameters from start to finish to see the full picture.\n\nConsider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' - 4y = 4e^{2x}\n$$\n\nfor which the corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1 = e^{2x}$ and $y_2 = e^{-2x}.$ \n\nLet’s work through this example using the method of variation of parameters.\n\nWe seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = u_1 y_1 + u_2 y_2\n$$\n\nsubject to the standard constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=4{e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{2x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $W \\neq 0,$ the functions are linearly independent. \n\nSubstituting $y_1, y_2$ and their derivatives into our constraints gives the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{2x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}=4{e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=4{e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by solving for $u_2'$ from the second equation to get\n\n$$\nu_2' = -\\dfrac{u_1'e^{2x}}{e^{-2x}}=-u_1'e^{4x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ 2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}-2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}){e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ 2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =4{e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nu_2' = -(1)e^{4x} = -e^{4x}.\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $u_1'$ and $u_2'$ with respect to $x$ (taking constants as $0$):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x=x, \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int -{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(x){e}^{2x}+(-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4x}){e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is $y = y_c + y_p,$ where $y_c = C_1 e^{2x} + C_2 e^{-2x}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =({C}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{C}_{2}{e}^{-2x})+(x{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({C}_{1}-\\frac{1}{4}){e}^{2x}+{C}_{2}{e}^{-2x}+x{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, replacing constants $C_1 - \\dfrac{1}{4}$ with $A$ and $C_2$ with $B,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y(x) = A e^{2x} + B e^{-2x} + xe^{2x}}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148652 #edb/ref 1065 14604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148652 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5e400cb0-5143-58c7-b8cf-1bf5a381f147" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148652 #edb/ref 1067 "The Variation of Parameters Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148653 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, consider the second-order linear inhomogeneous equation\n\n$$\ny'' - 3y' + 2y = f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere we assume that the forcing function $f$ is not identically zero.\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\ny'' - 3y' + 2y = 0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y = e^{\\lambda x}$ into the equation and solving the resulting quadratic in $\\lambda,$ we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = Ae^x + Be^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants, and the fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = e^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the Wronskian \n\n![](images/e5/e5fd444b69d4c3b0e7acc93217dc44dd3b6c4042da7fda41b6bb38d1b36336ba.svg)\n\nis nonzero for all $x,$ and so the set \n\n$$\nS = \\{y_1, y_2\\}\n$$\n\n \nis a linearly independent set of fundamental solutions. \n\nNow, suppose we wish to seek a particular solution using variation of parameters. By allowing the constants $A$ and $B$ to vary, we can introduce new functions $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x),$ and seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1y_1+ u_2y_2.\n$$\n\nBut we have a problem! Differentiating $y_p=u_1y_1+u_2y_2,$ we get terms involving $u'_1$ and $u'_2.$ Substituting into the ODE produces a single equation involving both $u'_1$ and $u'_2,$ so we need a second equation.\n\nTo help with this issue, we will seek a solution that satisfies the following constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis constraint for $y_p'$ does **not** come from differentiating $y_p.$ Rather, it is a condition that we force so that we obtain a solvable system of equations. We'll go over its justification in more detail at the end of the lesson.\n\nLet's go ahead and summarize everything we talked about, as well as a strategy for finding the unknown functions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148653 #edb/ref 1065 14605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148653 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e5fdbafa-19fb-5043-b20f-e4031ce98897" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148653 #edb/ref 1067 "Variation of Parameters for Second-Order Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148654 #edb/ref 1064 "We wish to find a particular solution $y_p$ of the equation\n\n$$\ny'' - 3y' + 2y = f(x)\n$$\n\nwith the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = e^{2x}.\n$$\n\nUsing variation of parameters, we seek functions $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{p}^{′} & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's outline our strategy for calculating $u_1$ and $u_2$.\n\n- **Step 1**: To ensure the expression for $y_p'$ above is valid, we must first impose the condition $u_1' y_1 + u_2' y_2 = 0.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we differentiate $y_p'$ to find $y_p'',$ substitute it into the differential equation, and simplify. This yields the second condition $u_1' y_1' + u_2' y_2' = f(x).$\n\n- **Step 3**: We solve the resulting system of two equations for the derivatives $u_1'$ and $u_2',$ then integrate to find $u_1$ and $u_2.$\n\nIn this lesson, we will practice each step of the process.\n\nLet's begin by seeing an example where we derive the system of equations for $u_1'$ and $u_2'.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148654 #edb/ref 1065 14606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148654 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "89fde0ba-2e02-599e-b324-dca21c49e2c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148654 #edb/ref 1067 "A Summary" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148655 #edb/ref 1064 "In variation of parameters for a second-order linear equation, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = u_1y_1 + u_2y_2,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are unknown functions.\n\nAt first, it may seem natural to differentiate $y_p$ directly:\n\n$$\ny_p' = (u_1y_1+u_2y_2)' = u_1'y_1 + u_1y_1' + u_2'y_2 + u_2y_2'.\n$$\n\nThis expression contains the *extra terms* $u_1'y_1$ and $u_2'y_2,$ which will also appear (after differentiating again) in the expression for $y_p''$.\n\nIf we substitute $y=y_p$ into the differential equation, we will obtain *one* equation involving *two* unknown derivatives $u_1'$ and $u_2',$ so we need a second equation to determine them.\n\nTo create a second equation, we *choose* to impose the condition\n\n$$\nu_1'y_1 + u_2'y_2 = 0.\n$$\n\nThis is not a property of $u_1$ and $u_2$ that we already know, but it is an additional constraint that we enforce to make the system solvable.\n\nNow, observe what happens to the formula for $y_p'.$ Starting from the product rule expansion,\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1'y_1 + u_1y_1' + u_2'y_2 + u_2y_2',\n$$\n\nand using the constraint $u_1'y_1+u_2'y_2=0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2y_2'.\n$$\n\nSo, the commonly stated \"derivative condition\"\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2y_2'\n$$\n\nis simply the product-rule derivative of $y_p$ *after* we impose $u_1'y_1+u_2'y_2=0.$\n\nThis constraint has two key benefits:\n\n1\\. *It produces a solvable system.* Substituting $y_p$ into the differential equation gives one equation in $u_1'$ and $u_2'.$  The constraint $u_1'y_1 + u_2'y_2=0$ gives a second equation.\n\n2\\. *It simplifies the computation of $y_p''.$* Since the $u_1'y_1$ and $u_2'y_2$ terms vanish, the expression for $y_p''$ becomes simpler, and the final system for $u_1'$ and $u_2'$ is easier to solve.\n\nThis is why variation of parameters uses the constraint $u_1'y_1 + u_2'y_2 = 0,$ and why it leads to the simplified expression $y_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2y_2'.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148655 #edb/ref 1065 14608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148655 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c03cbb8e-4fdf-5cf9-9785-b3fe55ecb63f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148655 #edb/ref 1067 "Justifying the Derivative Condition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148752 #edb/ref 1064 "In this topic, we'll learn how to apply the method of *variation of parameters* (VOP) to second-order linear differential equations. This is a powerful method because it applies to any second-order linear equation with continuous coefficients and is especially helpful when finding a particular solution is difficult using other methods.\n\nTo motivate the discussion, let's start by reviewing the variation of parameters method for first-order equations:\n\n$$\ny'(x) + p(x)y(x) = q(x)\n$$\n\nIn general, its solution has the form\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x),\n$$\n\nwhere the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ is the general solution of the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\ny'_c(x) + p(x)y_c(x) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A e^{-\\int p(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nAs we've seen, to seek a particular solution $y_p(x)$ using variation of parameters, we allow the constant $A$ to vary. Thus, in our particular solution, we replace the constant $A$ with a function $u(x){:}$\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x) e^{-\\int p(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting this ansatz into the original equation and solving the resulting differential equation for $u(x)$ yields the required particular solution.\n\nWe can apply VOP to second-order (and even higher-order) equations; the general idea is the same, but the situation is more complex because the complementary solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, not one! Thus, the variation of parameters ansatz will contain *two* unknown functions, and we will need additional conditions to determine them.\n\nBefore we dive into the second-order case, suppose we denote the function $y_1$ as\n\n$$\ny_1 = e^{-\\int p(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we can write our variation of parameters ansatz as\n\n$$\ny_p = u_1(x) y_1(x).\n$$\n\nThe function $y_1$ is a fundamental solution of the homogeneous equation. Since it is nonzero for all $x,$ it spans the solution space of the first-order homogeneous equation. In other words, the set\n\n$$\nS = \\{y_1\\}\n$$\n\nis (trivially) linearly independent. \n\nAs we'll see, thinking of the variation of parameters ansatz in terms of products of unknown functions and *linearly independent* fundamental solutions will help us when we apply variation of parameters to higher-order equations.\n\nLet's now consider a concrete example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148752 #edb/ref 1065 4093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148752 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0a574183-f2d1-59ce-b704-29526e664aa8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186148752 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308760 #edb/ref 1080 "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3{u}_{2}{e}^{3x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308760 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308760 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01ebcfc9-08a7-5b03-9884-e85211d1bf24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308903 #edb/ref 1080 "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=g(x)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308903 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308903 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14cbae37-20ec-54be-9725-035f89edbf1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308922 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308922 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308922 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1692dda9-7adf-5ade-800a-ff942ec75909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308931 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921863