}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between ${u}_{1}^{′},{u}_{2}^{′},$ and $g(x):$\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311879 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "82772dd4-65b4-593f-86b1-6fab92304fdd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311879 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{-3x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-4x})^{′}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+12y & =g(x) \\\\ ({y}_{p}^{′})^{′}+7{y}_{p}^{′}+12{y}_{p} & =g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our expressions for ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}_{1}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-4x})^{′})}^{′}+7({u}_{1}({e}^{-3x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-4x})^{′})+12({u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}) & =g(x) \\\\ {(-3{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x})}^{′}-21{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}-28{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}+12{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+12{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x} & =g(x) \\\\ -3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}+9{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}-21{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}-28{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x}+12{u}_{1}{e}^{-3x}+12{u}_{2}{e}^{-4x} & =g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group all terms proportional to ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}{{(9{e}^{-3x}-21{e}^{-3x}+12{e}^{-3x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{2}{{(16{e}^{-4x}-28{e}^{-4x}+12{e}^{-4x})}_{⏟}}_{0}-3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the expressions in parentheses are both zero. Therefore, the equation reduces to\n\n$$\n-3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=g(x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311898 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311898 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311898 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311898 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311898 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337938" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311898 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311898 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+6y={e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{3x}.$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311898 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c699add8-0039-500d-8f77-5f64053c7109" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311898 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{3x})^{′}.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}$ by differentiating ${y}_{p}$ and equating this expression to our constraint on ${y}_{p}^{′}.$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{3x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{2x})}^{′}+{({u}_{2}{e}^{3x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{1}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}({e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3{u}_{2}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3{u}_{2}{e}^{3x} & ={u}_{1}({e}^{2x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3{u}_{2}{e}^{3x} & =2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}+3{u}_{2}{e}^{3x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3{u}_{2}{e}^{3x}} & =\\cancel{2{u}_{1}{e}^{2x}}+\\cancel{3{u}_{2}{e}^{3x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{3x}=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311934 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311934 #edb/ref 1069 "q-338337" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311934 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311934 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-9y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311934 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2031b21a-a65a-54df-8dd6-56c0bb89f676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311934 #edb/ref 1074 "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{-3x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}({e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-3{e}^{-3x})-(3{e}^{3x})({e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =-3-3 \\\\  & =-6\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{3x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-3x})}^{′}={x}^{2} 